            body: "\n<span class=\"ln\"><u>Real World eg.</u></span>\n<span class=\"ln\">Suppose you owe ₹500 to a friend</span>\n<span class=\"ln indent\">(debt = − ₹500)</span>\n<span class=\"ln\">If my friend says \"I remove ₹200 from my debt\", that means:</span>\n<span class=\"ln indent\">−500 − (−200) = −500 + 200</span>\n<span class=\"ln indent\">= −300</span>\n<span class=\"ln\">Your debt reduces from 500 to 300 — you are ₹200 better off.</span>\n<span class=\"ln\"><u>Insight:</u></span>\n<span class=\"ln\">Subtracting a debt (−200) is same as gaining ₹200.</span>\n<span class=\"ln\">Subtracting a negative = Adding a positive</span>\n<span class=\"ln center\"><span class=\"box\">a − (−b) = a + b</span></span>\n",
            plainText: "4. Explain, using a real-world example of debt, why subtracting a negative number is the same as adding a positive number (e.g., 10 − (−5) = 15). Real World eg. Suppose you owe ₹500 to a friend (debt = − ₹500) If my friend says \"I remove ₹200 from my debt\", that means: −500 − (−200) = −500 + 200 = −300 Your debt reduces from 500 to 300 — you are ₹200 better off. Insight: Subtracting a debt (−200) is same as gaining ₹200. Subtracting a negative = Adding a positive a − (−b) = a + b",
