            body: "<span class=\"ln\">Construction: Draw AB = 5 cm. At A construct ∠A = 100°. With A as centre and radius 4 cm, locate C on the ray and join BC.</span><div style=\"margin:12px 0 18px;display:flex;justify-content:center;overflow-x:auto;\"><svg height=\"220\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;background:#fffdf3;border:1px solid #d8cfb5;border-radius:8px;\" viewbox=\"0 0 520 220\" width=\"520\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><line stroke=\"#213575\" stroke-width=\"3\" x1=\"82\" x2=\"430\" y1=\"178\" y2=\"178\"></line><line stroke=\"#213575\" stroke-width=\"3\" x1=\"82\" x2=\"188\" y1=\"178\" y2=\"72\"></line><line stroke=\"#213575\" stroke-width=\"3\" x1=\"430\" x2=\"188\" y1=\"178\" y2=\"72\"></line><circle cx=\"311\" cy=\"185\" fill=\"none\" r=\"190\" stroke=\"#b3372c\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" stroke-width=\"2\"></circle><circle cx=\"311\" cy=\"185\" fill=\"#b3372c\" r=\"4\"></circle><text font-size=\"18\" x=\"74\" y=\"200\">B</text><text font-size=\"18\" x=\"430\" y=\"200\">C</text><text font-size=\"18\" x=\"183\" y=\"60\">A</text><text font-size=\"18\" x=\"320\" y=\"180\">O</text><text font-size=\"15\" x=\"176\" y=\"215\">obtuse triangle; O outside</text></svg></div><span class=\"ln\">Since ∠A = 100° &gt; 90°, ΔABC is an obtuse-angled triangle.</span><span class=\"ln\">Construct the perpendicular bisectors of two sides. Their intersection is the circumcentre O outside the triangle.</span><span class=\"ln\">Therefore, the circumcentre lies <span class=\"box\">outside</span> the triangle.</span>",
            plainText: "2. Draw ΔABC with AB = 5 cm, ∠A = 100°, AC = 4 cm. Draw the circumcircle of ΔABC. Is the centre inside or outside the triangle? Construction: Draw AB = 5 cm. At A construct ∠A = 100°. With A as centre and radius 4 cm, locate C on the ray and join BC.BCAOobtuse triangle; O outside Since ∠A = 100° > 90°, ΔABC is an obtuse-angled triangle.Construct the perpendicular bisectors of two sides. Their intersection is the circumcentre O outside the triangle.Therefore, the circumcentre lies outside the triangle.",
