        questions: [
          {
            id: "q1",
            number: "1",
            head: "<span class=\"qnum\">1</span><span class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"ln\">Find the length of the chord of a circle where the radius is 7 cm and perpendicular distance is 6 cm.</span></span>",
            body: "<span class=\"ln\">Given, radius r = 7 cm and perpendicular distance d = 6 cm.</span><span class=\"ln\">Half of chord = √(r² − d²)</span><span class=\"ln\">= √(7² − 6²) = √13 cm.</span><span class=\"ln\">Therefore, length of chord = 2√13 = <span class=\"box\">2√13 cm</span> Ans.</span>",
            plainText: "1. Find the length of the chord of a circle where the radius is 7 cm and perpendicular distance is 6 cm. Given, radius r = 7 cm and perpendicular distance d = 6 cm.Half of chord = √(r² − d²)= √(7² − 6²) = √13 cm.Therefore, length of chord = 2√13 = 2√13 cm Ans.",
          },
          {
            id: "q2",
            number: "2",
            head: "<span class=\"qnum\">2</span><span class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"ln\">Explain why the following statement is true: If the perpendicular distance of a chord from the centre is d and the radius is r, then the chord length is 2√(r² − d²).</span></span>",
            body: "<span class=\"ln\">Let M be the midpoint of the chord.</span><span class=\"ln\">The perpendicular from the centre to the chord bisects it.</span><span class=\"ln\">So, OM = d and half chord = <span class=\"f\"><span class=\"n\">AB</span><span class=\"d\">2</span></span>.</span><span class=\"ln\">By Baudhāyana–Pythagoras,</span><span class=\"ln\">r² = d² + <span class=\"f\"><span class=\"n\">AB²</span><span class=\"d\">4</span></span></span><span class=\"ln\">⇒ <span class=\"f\"><span class=\"n\">AB²</span><span class=\"d\">4</span></span> = r² − d²</span><span class=\"ln\">⇒ AB = <span class=\"box\">2√(r² − d²)</span> Ans.</span>",
            plainText: "2. Explain why the following statement is true: If the perpendicular distance of a chord from the centre is d and the radius is r, then the chord length is 2√(r² − d²). Let M be the midpoint of the chord.The perpendicular from the centre to the chord bisects it.So, OM = d and half chord = AB2.By Baudhāyana–Pythagoras,r² = d² + AB²4⇒ AB²4 = r² − d²⇒ AB = 2√(r² − d²) Ans.",
          },
          {
            id: "q3",
            number: "★3",
            head: "<span class=\"qnum\">★3</span><span class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"ln\">In a circle, if the distance of chord AB from the centre is twice the distance of another chord CD from the centre, then can we conclude that CD = 2 AB? Give reasons for your answer.</span></span>",
            body: "<span class=\"ln\">No, we cannot conclude that CD = 2AB.</span><span class=\"ln\">For any chord, length = 2√(r² − d²).</span><span class=\"ln\">Thus chord length depends on the radius and the distance from the centre; it is not directly proportional to the distance.</span><span class=\"ln\">For example, taking r = 5 cm, d_AB = 2 cm and d_CD = 1 cm,</span><span class=\"ln\">AB = 2√21 cm while CD = 4√6 cm.</span><span class=\"ln\">Therefore, <span class=\"box\">CD ≠ 2AB</span>.</span>",
            plainText: "★3. In a circle, if the distance of chord AB from the centre is twice the distance of another chord CD from the centre, then can we conclude that CD = 2 AB? Give reasons for your answer. No, we cannot conclude that CD = 2AB.For any chord, length = 2√(r² − d²).Thus chord length depends on the radius and the distance from the centre; it is not directly proportional to the distance.For example, taking r = 5 cm, d_AB = 2 cm and d_CD = 1 cm,AB = 2√21 cm while CD = 4√6 cm.Therefore, CD ≠ 2AB.",
          },
        ],
